# <type>(<scope>): <subject>          <- imperative, lowercase, no trailing period, <=72 chars
#
# <body: why, not what. wrap at 72. blank line above.>
#
# <footers: BREAKING CHANGE: ... / Refs: ...>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Conventional Commits 1.0.0 — see CLAUDE.md "Commit messages"
#
# type:  feat     new capability (a service, resource, dashboard, load profile)
#        fix      corrects broken behavior or a misconfigured resource
#        perf     change whose point is an SLO/latency/throughput improvement
#        refactor restructure with no behavior or infrastructure change
#        test     k6 profiles, assertions, thresholds
#        docs     README, CLAUDE.md, specs, plans, recorded measurements
#        build    dependencies, Dockerfile, package.json
#        chore    tooling and housekeeping; nothing users or infra observe
#        revert   reverts a previous commit
#
# scope: the project directory, named for its scenario -- <platform>-<scenario>,
#        e.g. ecs-document-db, lambda-concurrency-limit. Never bare "ecs"/"lambda":
#        there will be several scenarios per platform. Append the layer with a
#        slash when it matters: ecs-document-db/terraform. Or repo/docs.
#
# BREAKING CHANGE = requires destroy/recreate rather than an in-place update,
# or changes an interface another project's config depends on. Mark it with
# `!` after the scope AND a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer explaining the migration.
#
# Examples:
#   feat(ecs-document-db/terraform): provision fargate service behind an alb
#   perf(lambda-concurrency-limit): raise reserved concurrency to 200 for the p99 slo
#   test(ecs-document-db/k6): add constant-load profile at 400 rps
#   docs(ecs-document-db): record before/after slo attainment for autoscaling
#   fix(lambda-concurrency-limit/terraform)!: replace rds instance for iam auth
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
